SSC discovery can take tens of seconds.  g_bus_own_name() publishes the name
before name_acquired_handler() finishes discovery, so clients can cache
HasAccelerometer=false and never receive an update: the normal notification
path only targets clients which already claimed a sensor, but a compositor
cannot know that it should claim while its cached availability is false.

Broadcast the complete property set after initial discovery.  D-Bus properties
are public already; later measurements remain restricted to claimed clients.

diff --git a/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c b/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
index 2da80bb..66490a7 100644
--- a/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
+++ b/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
@@ -238,8 +238,6 @@ send_dbus_event_for_client (SensorData     *data,
 	GVariantBuilder props_builder;
 	GVariant *props_changed = NULL;
 
-	g_return_if_fail (destination_bus_name != NULL);
-
 	g_variant_builder_init (&props_builder, G_VARIANT_TYPE ("a{sv}"));
 
 	if (mask & PROP_HAS_ACCELEROMETER) {
@@ -784,8 +782,13 @@ name_acquired_handler (GDBusConnection *connection,
 	if (!any_sensors_left (data))
 		goto bail;
 
-	send_dbus_event (data, PROP_ALL);
-	send_dbus_event (data, PROP_ALL_COMPASS);
+	/*
+	 * Slow backends may finish discovery after clients have already cached
+	 * the empty property set.  A NULL destination broadcasts the availability
+	 * update; per-reading updates remain unicast to clients that claimed them.
+	 */
+	send_dbus_event_for_client (data, NULL, PROP_ALL);
+	send_dbus_event_for_client (data, NULL, PROP_ALL_COMPASS);
 	return;
 
 bail:
